🔹 Canonical vs Compact Constructor (Java Records)

Dono record constructors hain — kaam related hai, style alag.


═══════════════════════════════════════
1) Canonical Constructor
═══════════════════════════════════════

Kya hai?
→ Record ka MAIN / FULL constructor.
→ Parameters EXACTLY record components jaisi (same names, same order).
→ Compiler usually auto-generate karta hai.

Example record:
  record Person(String name, int age) {}

Auto-generated canonical:
  public Person(String name, int age) {
      this.name = name;
      this.age = age;
  }

Call:
  new Person("Alice", 30);   // → canonical constructor

Agar khud likho (rarely needed — jab normalize/copy karna ho):

  record Person(String name, int age) {
      // Explicit CANONICAL constructor — params MUST match header
      public Person(String name, int age) {
          this.name = name.trim();
          this.age = age;
      }
  }

Rules:
- Params = components (same type + order)
- Aap KHUD assign karte ho: this.name = name;
- Extra constructors MUST isko call karein via this(...)


═══════════════════════════════════════
2) Compact Constructor
═══════════════════════════════════════

Kya hai?
→ Canonical ka SHORT form — sirf logic/validation likhte ho.
→ Parameters list NAHI likhte (header se aa jati hain).
→ Field assignment compiler AFTER your body auto karta hai.

Syntax:
  record Person(String name, int age) {
      public Person {                    // ← no (String name, int age)
          if (age < 0) {
              throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age cannot be negative");
          }
          // name / age already in scope
          // NO this.name = name;  ← compiler karega baad mein
      }
  }

Call same hi:
  new Person("Alice", 30);   // compact body chalti hai, phir fields assign

Typical use → validation / normalization BEFORE fields set:

  public Person {
      Objects.requireNonNull(name);
      name = name.trim();   // param reassign OK — assigned value field mein jayegi
      if (age < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("bad age");
  }


═══════════════════════════════════════
3) Side-by-side
═══════════════════════════════════════

|                    | Canonical                              | Compact                                |
|--------------------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| Params written?    | Yes: Person(String name, int age)      | No: Person { ... }                     |
| this.x = x ?       | YOU write                              | Compiler writes after body             |
| Best for           | Full control, rare manual assign       | Validation / normalize (common)        |
| Same call site?    | Yes — new Person("A", 30)              | Yes — new Person("A", 30)              |

Important:
- Compact = shortcut for the canonical constructor (replace karta hai, alag 2nd constructor nahi).
- Ek waqt pe ya to compact likho YA explicit canonical — dono ek saath nahi.


═══════════════════════════════════════
4) Extra constructors (overloads)
═══════════════════════════════════════

Extra constructors MUST eventually canonical/compact ko call karein:

  record Person(String name, int age) {
      public Person {                 // compact = canonical
          if (age < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("bad age");
      }

      public Person(String name) {    // overload
          this(name, 0);              // → canonical/compact
      }

      public Person() {
          this("Unknown", 0);
      }
  }


═══════════════════════════════════════
5) Dry run
═══════════════════════════════════════

  new Person("Bob", 25);
  1) Compact/canonical body chalti hai (validation)
  2) Fields assign: this.name = "Bob"; this.age = 25;
  3) Object ready → person.name() == "Bob"

  new Person("Bob", -1);
  1) Compact: age < 0 → exception
  2) Fields assign nahi hoti


🧠 Interview one-liner
“Canonical constructor is the all-args constructor matching record components; compact constructor is its short form
used mainly for validation without writing field assignments.”
